  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1208 AM CDT Sun Jul 12 2026\n\nOur area will have the potential for locally heavy rainfall and an \nassociated risk for flooding and flash flooding Monday through \nThursday. During the early part of the week an elongated, \nanomalously strong upper ridge will extend from the central/northern \nRockies east into the upper Midwest. An upper trough early Monday \nmorning will extend from Virgina and eastern Tennessee west-\nsouthwest into North Texas. The part of this trough extending to \nwestern North Texas will be rather weak. The trailing (and weak) \nportion of a cold frontal boundary Monday morning will be positioned \neast-west across the Big Country. The aforementioned trough axis and \nresidual east/west frontal boundary/surface trough are forecast to \nshift south across at least our central counties during the day. \nWith some surface convergence, weak support aloft and an uncapped \nairmass, scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected on Monday. The airmass will be very moist with precipitable \nwater values 1.6 to 2 inches (possibly a little higher in portions \nof our area). With slow-moving convection and the possibility for \nrepeat development, heavy rainfall in short periods of time will \nbring a threat for localized flooding/flash flooding. Monday will be \nthe beginning of a downward trend in temperatures, with highs \nexpected to be mostly in the upper 80s to lower 90s.  \n\nOn Tuesday the main focus for showers and thunderstorms looks to be \nin the southern half of our area, with a possibility for locally \nheavy rainfall and flooding with a very moist airmass and slow-\nmoving convection. Forecast highs Tuesday are in the mid to upper \n80s in our southern and central counties, and upper 80s to lower 90s \nin the Big Country. \n\nWednesday into Thursday an extensive and strong upper level ridge is \nforecast to be centered over South Dakota/Nebraska with extensions \neast-southeast into the middle Appalachians, and southwest across \nUtah into southern Nevada. To the south of this ridge a broad \ninverted trough is progged to move west across Texas and Oklahoma, \nand reach the New Mexico/Texas state line into northern Mexico \nThursday. Periodic disturbances will rotate around this inverted \ntrough, and may have weak closed lows at times (especially Thursday \nnear the Big Bend). With the airmass remaining moist and the \npossibility of better upper support, much of our area could have \nscattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms Wednesday into \nThursday, with the western half of our area the main focus on \nThursday. Locally heavy rainfall and flooding will continue to be a \npossibility. With increased cloud cover, highs Wednesday and \nThursday should be limited to the 80s to near 90. \n\nRain chances should decrease Friday as the inverted trough/low shift \nfarther west into New Mexico. Building heights aloft are indicated \nby the start of next weekend as part of an upper level ridge shifts \ninto Oklahoma and Arkansas. Temperatures will be increasing by that \ntime with highs reaching the lower to mid 90s in most of our central \nand northern counties Saturday.",
